markdown <<MARKDOWN
### 🧠 Internet Misconceptions Bingo

Check any statements you've heard from students, colleagues, or adults in your life.  
(You’re not marking what *you* believe — just what you’ve heard!)

<table>
  <tr>
    <td>
      <label>
        <input type="checkbox">
        WiFi and the Internet are the same thing.
      </label>
    </td>
    <td>
      <label>
        <input type="checkbox">
        The Internet is one big computer that everyone connects to.
      </label>
    </td>
    <td>
      <label>
        <input type="checkbox">
        The Web *is* the Internet (they’re just different names).
      </label>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>
      <label>
        <input type="checkbox">
        Data travels in a straight line from my device to the website.
      </label>
    </td>
    <td>
      <label>
        <input type="checkbox">
        Once a message is sent, it can’t be broken into smaller pieces.
      </label>
    </td>
    <td>
      <label>
        <input type="checkbox">
        If one router fails, the whole Internet goes down.
      </label>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>
      <label>
        <input type="checkbox">
        An IP address is just a username, like an email handle.
      </label>
    </td>
    <td>
      <label>
        <input type="checkbox">
        DNS is where all websites are stored.
      </label>
    </td>
    <td>
      <label>
        <input type="checkbox">
        HTTP is only used when you submit a form, not when loading pages.
      </label>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<br/>

::: details [💡 Reveal: What’s actually going on?]

**These are all misconceptions**, and many of them will show up in student thinking during this unit.

A few quick clarifications you can keep in your back pocket:

- **WiFi vs. Internet vs. Web**
  - WiFi = local wireless connection.
  - Internet = global network infrastructure (wires, routers, protocols).
  - Web = one service that runs on the Internet.

- **How data travels**
  - Data is broken into **packets** that can take **multiple paths** through **multiple routers**, and then get reassembled.

- **Redundancy**
  - If one router or path fails, other paths can still move data — that’s redundancy.

- **IP, DNS, HTTP**
  - **IP address** = numeric address for devices.
  - **DNS** = directory that maps names (like `example.com`) to IPs.
  - **HTTP** = the rules for how browsers and servers request and send web content.

As you teach the unit, you can listen for which of these boxes your students would “check” — and use activities and questions in Lessons 1–6 to help them revise those mental models.

:::
MARKDOWN
